<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#cumbric">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cumbric</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cumbric was the Brittonic Celtic language of the early medieval north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, spoken in the kingdom of Strathclyde and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumbria">Cumbria</a>. Closely related to Old Welsh, it is known mainly from place names and a few personal names and legal terms. It probably died out in the 11th or 12th century, giving way to Gaelic, English and Norse speech.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cumbric.jpg" alt="Cumbric" width="320" height="593" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: edited by Paul S, based on a map by Ian Macky · Attribution · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Language_in_Scotland_11th_Century.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
